 English strings. The source literals are already English, so almost nothing belongs here: a lookup that finds
 no entry returns the English text unchanged, which is what an English user should see.

 What does belong here is text that only exists once the other language is chosen — a Chinese sentence the code
 asks for by its English key, which would otherwise show through in an English interface. Nothing does today:
 the language menu names each language in that language's own letters, which needs no lookup. So this file is
 kept for the pairing, not for its contents — a lone `zh-Hans.lproj` under a synchronized root group is the one
 shape Xcode may fail to read as a variant group, and the English side is what makes the pair unambiguous.
